                                                                  9040 1451  431
EVALUATION OF THE RESONANCE PARAMETERS IN THE ENERGY RANGE 0 to   9040 1451  432
 4 keV - L.C. Leal and H. Derrien, ORNL(2005)                     9040 1451  433
                                                                  9040 1451  434
                                                                  9040 1451  435
  The resonance parameters were obtained from a sequential Bayes  9040 1451  436
analysis, with the computer code SAMMY, of an experimental data   9040 1451  437
base including Olsen(1) neutron transmission data(ORELA),         9040 1451  438
Schillebeeckx(2) capture data(GELINA), and Gunsing(3) capture     9040 1451  439
data(nTof) in the energy range 1 eV to 4 keV.  In the thermal     9040 1451  440
energy range the capture data of Chrien(4) and of Lundgreen(5)    9040 1451  441
were normalized to a value of 7.35 b recommended by Trkov(6) and  9040 1451  442
fitted by SAMMY along with the total cross section of Olsen(1) in 9040 1451  443
the energy range up to 1 eV.                                      9040 1451  444
  The contribution of the external resonances(negative energy     9040 1451  445
resonances and resonances at energies larger than 4 keV) was      9040 1451  446
obtained from two fictitious resonances, one at -2000 eV and the  9040 1451  447
other at 6000 eV.  The parameters of these resonances allow the   9040 1451  448
representation of the Olsen thick samples transmission data with  9040 1451  449
an accuracy of 1% on average, and a constant value of R'=9.686 fm 9040 1451  450
could be used over the entire energy range analyzed, in agreement 9040 1451  451
with Olsen(7) evaluated value. A ladder of 7 s-wave negative      9040 1451  452
energy resonances from -3 eV to -110 eV was used to help the fit  9040 1451  453
of the thermal energy range. The resonance at -3.52 eV could be   9040 1451  454
used to adjust the cross sections at 0.0253 eV.                   9040 1451  455
  The resonance set contain 244 s-wave resonances and 669 p-wave  9040 1451  456
resonances. The large s-wave resonances were identified from their9040 1451  457
shape; some other resonances were assigned s-wave because leading 9040 1451  458
to too large reduced neutron width when assigned p.  A large      9040 1451  459
number of resonances assigned p-wave are not seen in the          9040 1451  460
experimental data; they were used to obtain the agreement with    9040 1451  461
the Wigner distribution of the spacing and the Porter-Thomas      9040 1451  462
distribution of the reduced neutron width.  It can be shown that  9040 1451  463
a set of resonances that does not contain p-wave resonances of    9040 1451  464
reduced neutron width smaller than 1.6 meV calculated average     9040 1451  465
capture cross section too small by 0.5% in the energy range below 9040 1451  466
1 keV, and too small by about 3% in the energy range 3 to 4 keV.  9040 1451  467
  The prior values of the resonance parameters in the SAMMY fit   9040 1451  468
were those from the Olsen(7) evaluation with a constant value of  9040 1451  469
24.4 meV for the capture width of all the resonances. The value of9040 1451  470
24.4 meV was kept for the p-wave resonances. In the SAMMY fit the 9040 1451  471
capture width was allowed to vary for the large s-wave resonances 9040 1451  472
since for most of these resonances the capture area is sensitive  9040 1451  473
to the capture width. However, the average of the varied capture  9040 1451  474
agrees within 4% with the value of 24.4 meV.                      9040 1451  475
  The cross sections calculated at 0.0253 eV with the resonance   9040 1451  476
parameters are 20.40 b and 7.34 b respectively for the total and  9040 1451  477
capture cross sections compared to 20.38 b and 7.40 b calculated  9040 1451  478
from ENDF/B-VI.  The capture resonance integral in the energy     9040 1451  479
range 0.5 eV to 4000 eV is 81.74 b from the present evaluation and9040 1451  480
83.62 b from ENDF/B-VI, i.e 2.3% lower than ENDF/B-VI. The total  9040 1451  481
cross section calculated with the resonance parameters is not     9040 1451  482
consistent with Olsen experimental data in the energy range near  9040 1451  483
0.0253 eV due to the Bragg scattering effect in the measured total9040 1451  484
cross section.                                                    9040 1451  485
  The average capture cross sections calculated in several energy 9040 1451  486
ranges are compared to the ENDF/B-VI values in the following      9040 1451  487
table (SAMMY calculation):                                        9040 1451  488
