EVALUATION PROCEDURE                                              9040 1451  530
  The present evaluation was obtained as the best NJOY-compatible 9040 1451  531
description of the total and capture cross section in the URR.    9040 1451  532
  Generalized single level representation was used by following   9040 1451  533
the Froehner's (FITACS) approach [Fr00] approximated to be        9040 1451  534
compatible with the ENDF-6 SLBW [CS01]. The contribution of the   9040 1451  535
distant levels was accounted according to the standard description9040 1451  536
scheme [Fr00], but with distant level parameters (and consequently9040 1451  537
scattering radius) being energy dependent.                        9040 1451  538
  The energy dependence of the potential scattering radius R'(E)  9040 1451  539
was rigorously calculated from the OM shape elastic cross section 9040 1451  540
& neutron transmission coefficients, taken from EMPIRE [He05]     9040 1451  541
output [Ca05]. The corresponding TAB1 record is listed below.     9040 1451  542
The energy-dependence of level spacing was calculated according to9040 1451  543
the Gilbert-Cameron's level density formula. The energy-dependence9040 1451  544
of gamma width was calculated according to the GDR model.         9040 1451  545
  Average resonance parameters S0, S1, S2, Gg0, Gg1 were obtained 9040 1451  546
from a simultaneous fit to the experimental data: total cross     9040 1451  547
section data [Po81, Iw81, Ut66], capture cross section data [Bo04,9040 1451  548
Gu04, Po78], and renormalized capture data [Ma81, Ko81], with the 9040 1451  549
constraint to be consistent with the recent IAEA EMPIRE evaluation9040 1451  550
[Ca05]. In the least square adjustment, D0 was kept fixed at 17.4 9040 1451  551
eV according to the latest results obtained at n_TOF [Gu05]. The  9040 1451  552
infinitely-dilute cross sections were determined with the RECENT  9040 1451  553
code [Cu05], which (in contrast to NJOY [Ma00]) can process the   9040 1451  554
ENDF-6 option of energy-dependent scattering radius.              9040 1451  555
  Average parameters:                                             9040 1451  556
                S (1E-4)      D (eV)*      Gg (meV)**             9040 1451  557
    s-wave      0.8784        17.40        23.55                  9040 1451  558
    p-wave      1.902                      22.66                  9040 1451  559
    d-wave      1.266                      23.55                  9040 1451  560
   *  Level spacing at the neutron separation energy of target+n. 9040 1451  561
   ** Radiation width at En=0 eV.                                 9040 1451  562
                                                                  9040 1451  563
  The above description refers to the "basic evaluation". Most    9040 1451  564
of the processing codes (e.g. NJOY) can not handle the energy     9040 1451  565
dependent scattering radius. The adopted solution was to make     9040 1451  566
a best "NJOY-compatible" approximation to the basic evaluation    9040 1451  567
by putting the point-wise infinitely-dilute cross-section data    9040 1451  568
into File 3 and use unresolved resonance parameters to calculate  9040 1451  569
self-shielding only.                                              9040 1451  570
  Considering that self-shielding effects are strongest at the    9040 1451  571
lower end of the energy range, it was verified that the constant- 9040 1451  572
scattering-radius approximation, with R'-value taken at En=4 keV, 9040 1451  573
retains the self-shielding factors practically intact. This       9040 1451  574
ensures that there is a consistency of both the cross sections and9040 1451  575
the self-shielding factors of the present evaluation with the     9040 1451  576
results obtained from the basic evaluation with energy-dependent  9040 1451  577
scattering radius.                                                9040 1451  578
  The evaluated infinitely-dilute cross sections (total, capture  9040 1451  579
and in-elastic) are fully consistent with those of EMPIRE. This   9040 1451  580
justified the unified use of the EMPIRE results in File 3         9040 1451  581
(infinitely-dilute cross sections) and File 4 (angular            9040 1451  582
distributions of secondary neutrons) from 4 to 100 keV.           9040 1451  583
